From: WelsonJS contributors
Subject: [PATCH] numbers.js: WelsonJS modifications

Turns the original numbers.js (numbers.js 0.7.0, the browserified build
https://github.com/numbers/numbers.js/blob/master/src/numbers.js) into the
global "numbers". app.js loads app/assets/js/numbers-0.7.0.js, which keeps
the upstream file that restoreAppAssets.ps1 downloads, and applies this
patch to it in memory (docs/require-patches.md). numbers.js is licensed
under the Apache License, Version 2.0; the file header records these
modifications.

- matrix.determinant: cofactor expansion along the first row
  (numbers/numbers.js#161, for issue #160).
- basic.powerMod: exact for every modulus up to 2^53 (no intermediate sum
  exceeds the modulus), and halves the exponent without the 32-bit >>
  operator (exponents of 2^31 and above gave wrong results).
  prime.millerRabin depends on it.
- basic.modInverse: returns the inverse in [0, m), not a negative number.
- calculus.adaptiveSimpson: splits each interval at its midpoint and passes
  the recursive arguments in order, with a rounding-error floor and a depth
  limit (it computed one Simpson step, or recursed without bound), and
  stops at once on a NaN error estimate.
- calculus.limit: approaches the point with shrinking steps, reports
  divergence, and uses "middle" by default (it evaluated f once at
  point +/- 1e-15).
- calculus.MonteCarlo: samples with random.sample (statistic.randomSample
  does not exist) and passes every coordinate to the function.
- generate.collatz: the result array is optional and returned, and the
  sequence is built in a loop.
- matrix.transpose: turns a one-dimensional array into a column vector.

The patched file is 84,126 bytes, SHA-384
sha384-gZ3b+UKp735rDlzLa+Okb+J0nBSwjht3tIsJGUY29H5qmouPW+diziv/wG5kwcxt: the
content that version 0.2.7.62 shipped as numbers-0.7.0.wsh.js.

Apply from the repository root to the original file saved as
app/assets/js/numbers-0.7.0.js:

    git apply patches/app/assets/js/numbers-0.7.0.js.patch

